# Offsite encrypted backup environment — TEMPLATE ONLY (quick/260807-ohd).
#
# The REAL counterpart of this file lives ON THE BOX at
#   ~/.config/devtools-backup/backup.env                (chmod 600)
# and is deliberately OUTSIDE infra/keygen/ because `deploy.sh` rsyncs that
# directory with --delete — anything new placed there would be wiped by the next
# deploy. Keeping it under ~/.config also keeps it out of `git status` entirely.
# (No path below assumes a particular user; the scripts resolve everything from
# $HOME / $XDG_CONFIG_HOME.)
#
# Create it ON THE BOX with the `cat >` + Ctrl-D pattern (only `cat > file`
# enters bash history; the pasted lines are stdin, not history). NEVER use
# `echo` / `export` for any value, never paste a value into a chat, never
# commit one:
#
#   umask 077
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/devtools-backup
#   cat > ~/.config/devtools-backup/backup.env
#   # ...paste the block below with YOUR values, then Ctrl-D
#   chmod 600 ~/.config/devtools-backup/backup.env
#
# `backup.sh` and `restore-test.sh` REFUSE to run if this file is missing or is
# not mode 600 (fail closed on a world-readable secret file).

# --- Cloudflare R2 (S3-compatible) ------------------------------------------
# From: Cloudflare Dashboard -> R2 -> Manage API Tokens -> Create API Token,
# permission "Object Read & Write", SCOPED TO THE ONE BUCKET (not all buckets).
# The secret access key is shown ONCE.
#
# These RCLONE_CONFIG_R2_* names are not arbitrary: rclone builds the whole `r2:`
# remote from them, so NO rclone.conf ever exists on disk and there is exactly
# one copy of the credentials on the box. The scripts keep them shell-local and
# hand them to rclone as a per-command prefix, so no other child process (gpg,
# docker, psql, curl) ever inherits the secret.
RCLONE_CONFIG_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<r2-access-key-id>
RCLONE_CONFIG_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<r2-secret-access-key>
# R2 overview -> "S3 API" endpoint for your account.
RCLONE_CONFIG_R2_ENDPOINT=https://<your-account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com

# The bucket the encrypted dumps land in, and the key prefix inside it.
# Objects are written as <BACKUP_PREFIX>/keygen-<UTC-timestamp>.sql.gz.gpg,
# each beside its own <...>.manifest.json.gz.gpg (what the artifact should
# restore to — the restore drill compares against that, never against live).
BACKUP_BUCKET=tinkerdev-backups
BACKUP_PREFIX=keygen-ce

# --- GPG symmetric passphrase ------------------------------------------------
# A mode-600 file containing ONLY the passphrase, on one line:
#   openssl rand -base64 32 > ~/.config/devtools-backup/gpg.pass
#   chmod 600 ~/.config/devtools-backup/gpg.pass
#
# !!! THE PASSPHRASE MUST ALSO LIVE IN THE OWNER'S PASSWORD MANAGER. !!!
# If the box dies holding the only copy, EVERY backup in R2 is permanently
# unrecoverable — you would have offsite ciphertext and no key. This is the
# single most likely way this whole pipeline ends up worthless.
#
# Defaults to ~/.config/devtools-backup/gpg.pass; only set it to override.
# BACKUP_GPG_PASSPHRASE_FILE=/some/other/path/gpg.pass

# --- healthchecks.io dead-man switches — BOTH REQUIRED -----------------------
# !!! THE SCRIPTS REFUSE TO RUN WITHOUT BOTH OF THESE. !!!
# An on-box log can only report failures the box is alive and healthy enough to
# write. It structurally cannot tell you "cron stopped firing", "the box is
# dead", or "the disk is full". Only an external check with a grace period can.
# A backup nobody is alerted about is not a backup — log-only is a FAILED setup,
# not a reduced-scope one.
#
# Two separate checks on purpose: "the backup did not run" and "the backup ran
# but no longer restores" are different alarms, and you want to tell them apart
# at 3am.
#   healthchecks.io -> Add Check -> `keygen-ce-backup`       period 1 day, grace 6h
HEALTHCHECK_PING_URL=https://hc-ping.com/<uuid-of-keygen-ce-backup>
#   healthchecks.io -> Add Check -> `keygen-ce-restore-test` period 1 day, grace 8h
HEALTHCHECK_RESTORE_PING_URL=https://hc-ping.com/<uuid-of-keygen-ce-restore-test>

# --- Retention ---------------------------------------------------------------
# BACKSTOP ONLY. The primary retention control is the R2 bucket's object
# lifecycle rule ("delete 30 days after creation"), which keeps working even if
# this box is destroyed. This script-side prune is deliberately WIDER (40 days)
# so it never races the lifecycle rule and can never eat a fresh backup.
# backup.sh REFUSES to start unless this is a whole number >= 35, because the
# value is a `rclone delete --min-age` argument.
BACKUP_RETENTION_BACKSTOP_DAYS=40

# --- Optional overrides (defaults are correct on the prod box) ---------------
# Every one of these is read by the scripts; they are listed here so the full
# surface is in one place. Uncomment only to change a default.
#
# COMPOSE_FILE=~/devtools/infra/keygen/compose.yaml  # live stack, backup.sh only
# BACKUP_MIN_BYTES=100000     # reject a suspiciously small plaintext dump
# PG_DUMP_TIMEOUT=600         # seconds; caps a wedged pg_dump under cron
# BACKUP_RCLONE_TIMEOUT=600   # seconds; caps any single rclone invocation.
#                             # (NOT RCLONE_TIMEOUT — rclone reads RCLONE_* as
#                             #  flags and would choke on a unit-less value.)
# RESTORE_TEST_TIMEOUT=900    # seconds; cap on the chained restore validation
# PG_READY_TIMEOUT=60         # seconds to wait for the throwaway postgres
# STALE_SWEEP_AGE=3600        # age (s) at which a throwaway container or a
#                             # work dir abandoned by a SIGKILLed run is swept
#
# BACKUP_ENV cannot live in here (it is the path OF this file). Set it in the
# environment to point the scripts at an alternative env file for testing:
#   BACKUP_ENV=/tmp/test.env ~/devtools/infra/keygen/backup.sh --list
